---
/**
 * Server rendering and hydration (ADR-005).
 *
 * §10 lists SSR and hydration as required guides. The content here is the ADR's
 * decision: two registration paths, chosen by the consumer, with the lazy path for
 * SSR.
 */
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout title="Server rendering and hydration" description="How Santaka UI registers custom elements under SSR.">
  <h1>Server rendering and hydration</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A custom element tag in server output is inert HTML until something calls <code>customElements.define</code>.
    Santaka UI gives you two paths, and the choice is about <em>when</em> registration happens.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>The eager path — the default</h2>
    <pre><code>import "@santaka-ui/web-components"</code></pre>
    <p>
      Importing the package registers every element. This is the documented path for plain HTML, React, Vue, Svelte, and
      Angular where the tag is known at build time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The lazy path — for SSR</h2>
    <pre><code>{`import { defineCustomElements } from "@santaka-ui/web-components/loader"

defineCustomElements()`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The loader defines nothing on import, which is the property that makes it safe on a server: if it registered at
      import time, the server pass would upgrade the tags and the client would then see a different tree than the server
      produced — a hydration mismatch.
    </p>
    <p>
      The consumer fixture asserts this: importing the loader must leave <code>customElements.get("sk-button")</code>
      undefined until <code>defineCustomElements()</code> is called.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Register after hydration</h2>
    <p>
      Whichever path you choose, the call that defines the elements has to happen <em>after</em> the framework has
      hydrated the server's markup. Santaka UI components use scoped styles and no shadow root, so an upgraded element
      renders into its own light DOM — it inserts nodes into the very tree the framework is walking. A framework that
      hydrates by matching the server's nodes against a template then finds a tree that no longer matches.
    </p>
    <p>
      React has always done the right thing here, which is why the Next.js example defines the elements inside an
      effect: effects run after hydration (ADR-005). Svelte makes the mistake visible instead of silent — hydrating a
      tree that changed throws, and Svelte answers by clearing the target and mounting the application again, so the
      page looks correct while the server rendering is gone. The Svelte example measures both orders and asserts the
      difference (ADR-021).
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`// client entry — the call order is the whole point
hydrate(App, { target: document.getElementById("app") })
void defineCustomElements()   // after hydration, never before`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Svelte</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/santaka-ui/santaka-ui/tree/main/examples/svelte-vite">Svelte example</a> serves a
      real server-rendered page from a small Node server and hydrates it in the browser. Its tests assert that
      hydration reused the server's nodes rather than replacing them, that props and the element's own
      <code>sk-value-change</code> event reach the application, and that all six form controls submit through the
      browser's own <code>FormData</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>@santaka-ui/svelte</code> ships no components. Svelte's compiler already writes a prop as a property when
      the element declares it and as an attribute when it does not, so the package supplies what Svelte cannot: the
      <code>./elements</code> types (so a misspelled attribute fails <code>svelte-check</code> instead of being dropped)
      and the theme store this section's pre-paint snippet belongs to. SvelteKit's own server entry is not covered by
      that example, because its peer requirements need a TypeScript major the workspace does not pin (ADR-021).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Next.js</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/santaka-ui/santaka-ui/tree/main/examples/nextjs">Next.js example</a> proves the
      path end to end: the server renders every shipped tag with its public attributes, defines nothing in any server
      chunk, upgrades each element through the lazy loader, applies the scoped styles, and hydrates with no mismatch
      errors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What is safe on a server</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Rendering the tags with their public attributes — they are inert HTML until upgraded.</li>
      <li>Importing the tokens and the component CSS — neither touches the DOM.</li>
      <li>Importing the loader — it defines nothing at import.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What is not safe is importing the eager entry in a server bundle, or calling <code>defineCustomElements()</code> there.</p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-inline-size: 65ch;
  }
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border: var(--docs-stroke) solid var(--docs-edge);
  }
</style>
